I think you're missing the point.  This may be different in the UK, but so
far here in the US, public resources are just that--public.  Students may
not pay the majority of taxes that get diverted to the public University
system, but they do pay some taxes and more importantly they are citizens
of their various states, which means that they are part of the group that
*owns* the computer through the university.  The President of the
University does not own the computer, the system administrator does not
own the computer, the people of the state own the computer and they should
expect to be able to use it in any manner they see fit.  Perhaps more
importantly, at many Universities (such as the one I attend) students pay
a specific fee along with their tuition that is used for computer
programs.  I don't think it's unreasonable that they should therefore
expect to use the resources that they are explicitly paying for as they
desire to.

