In article <2pbpfi$i2r@senator-bedfellow.mit.edu>,
Rob Reilly <rreilly@ATHENA.MIT.EDU> wrote:
>I would also point out that it's an interesting point that there are
>a number of entities which establish policy/administer computing
>facilities. 
>
>What's your opinion about the (I preassume) fact that there are a
>number of entities setting policies for computers system on the
>campus?  

I think that the diversity of computing policies is based in the 
diversity of our systems.  One should not necessarily have the
same policy for the "undergraduate general-purpose noodle-around"
system as one has for the "faculty and graduate student big-honking-
research-only" system.  A machine designed to support 50 users may
not be able to support the same policies (or levels of privilege,
perhaps) as one designed to support 500 users; for that matter, what 
about all those PCs?  Those are computing systems too, right?

There are certainly some issues which apply almost globally; user
privacy and system security issues are often placed in this cate-
gory.  However, attempts to establish a global computing policy
can cause more harm than good, in light of the system-specific
issues.  Any good policy will respect the differences among its
computing facilities; some leeway must be given to individual
facilities to craft policies that meet their needs/obligations.  

Now, I will say that these 'subsidiary' policies should all be
justified to the same group that writes/approves the campus-wide
policy, in order to maintain the tenets of the global policy. If, 
for instance, I want to restrict games/MUD/IRC on a particular 
system but allow them on another, I would have no problems with
justifying that decision to the campus policy group.  This seems 
(to me, anyway) to parallel the policy by which I must seek per-
mission from the appropriate authority before taking action against 
a user (e.g., search files, suspend access, etc.)

In conclusion, campus-wide policies which affirm the basic ideas
of computing privileges/responsibilities are a Good Thing; however, 
such policies should recognize and accomodate the need for site- 
or facility-specific additions to (or extensions of) that policy. 
We often criticize policies which we consider 'overly broad' or
'overly vague;' however, we need to realize that an overly specific 
policy can cause problems as well.

--Wes

