In article <15A37A6EB7@chuck.stchas.edu>, CBALLANTYNE@chuck.stchas.edu (Carol Ballantyne) writes:
> Has anyone developed a policy for students on the Internet? primarily 
> at the community college level with no on campus housing? We are 
> interested in developing an interim policy until we have dial access 
> for students. Thank you in advance for your assistance

All 20k+ students at Ball State may have VAX accounts, if they wish,
and through the VAX Cluster have complete access to the Internet.  We
do not police that beyond controlling the non-academic use of MUDDs
and  similar things in public computer labs.  Complaints against
student users are investigated by my office, and any restriction of
privileges is reviewed with the Office of the Dean of Students after
discussion with the user.  (No flames about complaints against faculty 
and staff users, please.  The topic here is students.)  All students
are subject to a student behavior policy passed by the Student Senate,
the University Senate, and the Board of Trustees.  We currently also
provide a bank of 40 14.4k inbound modems for student access to our 
ethernet backbone.  These are currently unrestricted, but we are
considering better ways to manage this limited resource to maximize
availability to students for academic uses.

Herbert Stahlke
Associate Director
University Computing Services
Ball State University

