In article <15ABEA56A0@chuck.stchas.edu>, CBALLANTYNE@chuck.stchas.edu (Carol Ballantyne) writes:
> I am interested in getting any input available on software 
> development-- who owns it? when grants are involved does it belong to 
> the individual? etc. Thank you in advance for your assistance.Carol E. Ballantyne

At Ball State University, the university generally owns copyright on
all intellectual properties produced by persons working for the 
university and using university facilities and equipment, including
projects supported by grants made to the university.  Exceptions
have included cases where the developer(s) provided their own 
facilities and equipment and did not receive assigned time, funding,
or personnel/technical support from the university.

Our royalties policy, briefly, states that the first $1500 in
royalties is divided by the authors/developers as they agree, then the
university may recover costs agreed to by the university and the
authors/developers, and after cost recovery 75% of the royalties go to
the authors and 25% to the Office of Academic Research and Sponsored
Programs to support other such projects.

