naively thought they were getting the "whole story".

Perhaps because of his "primary role" in the killing, Clayton Matchee
tried to kill himself in prison and has been left with severe brain damage.
Canadians who knew about Matchee through the American media,
have speculated that since Matchee could never go to trial, and
since "someone must pay", Elvin Kyle Brown was selected as the scape-goat.


One wonders what "dirty secrets" the courts and government are hiding behind
the Homolka publication ban.  It is clear the motivation has little
at all to do with the claimed desire for a "fair trial for Teale".
The highly unusual elimination of the preliminary trial is
evidence of that.

Canadians deserve the right to scrutinize the decision being made
in their judicial system.  In many cases, even if a publication ban
is eventually lifted, it is just too late.

