Message-ID: <199404252359.TAA01946@eff.org>

>On Sun, 24 Apr 1994 18:55:32 -0400 Ben Chi said:

>Now that's beginning to get to the heart of the matter.  The only trick
>now is to figure out how to decide how expensive is too expensive and
>how useful is not very useful.

We at Northwestern don't have an official written policy either, but I've
been faced with the problem for awhile, and have, like Carl, piggy-backed on
other established policy in our networked environment.  I'm glad Carl
brought up the intellectual freedom issues first, as they are key.

For listserv creation here at NU, we have followed the pattern as set by
NUInfo, our cwis: we allow list creation for "officially-recognized" groups
on campus, leaving the definition of what is "official" up to other bodies
(e.g., is the office that wants a list listed in the phone book, or
officially recognized by the Student Activities Board, etc.).  We also set
up lists for any academic class that wants one.  We've done this for classes
and organizations, and have currently about 130 lists running; we are not
"pushing" this service, but also directing folks to usenet newsgroups as
well -- an alternative in some situations, and more efficient for the
network.  We've turned down requests from a few students who come in to ask
for a list "for me and a few of my friends;" there is overhead with every
list created, and as it stands we may have to become more restrictive than
we are at present, just due to the machine issues.

I'd welcome comments on this policy from cwis-ers.  We've not gotten totally
inundated with mail yet, and this is I think a problem that many
institutions are facing.


