In article <2p3kl9$52b@masala.cc.uh.edu> sears@uh.edu (Paul S. Sears) writes:
>From: sears@uh.edu (Paul S. Sears)
>Subject: Question:  Legal Responsibility vs Privacy
>Date: 20 Apr 1994 16:18:49 GMT

>I have a situation that has developed and it had boiled down to "possible"  
>legal responsibility vs a user's right to privacy and we have some  
>questions.  Here is the background of the situation:

>A report generated a list of all users disk consumption on one of our  
>servers.  All in all, things were normal (usages in the range of 5-20M per  
>user), except that we had noticed that a user was consuming more that 150M  
>of disk space.  We only know the amount of disk usage, not the contents of  
>the account.  Ethically and legally we are obligated to protect the privacy  
>of the user in question (we have thus far), HOWEVER, AFTER TALKING 
>WITH SOME OTHER USERS (WHO INDIRECTLY KNOW THE USER IN QUESTION), IT 
>SEEMS APPARENT THAT THE ACOUNT MIGHT BE USED FOR ILLEGAL PURPOSES (SUSPICION 
>OF SOFTWARE PIRACY).  HOWEVER, WE HAVE NO EVIDENCE TO SUPPORT OUR SUSPICIONS, 
>OTHER THAN WHAT WE HAVE "HEARD" IN THE LAB.  Basically, to prove illegal 

note: emphasis in UPPERCASE added by Brian Reithel


>activity by  the user, we have to violate the user's privacy -- something we 
>are not  willing to do at this time...

>So, my question is three-fold:

>1)  To what extent can we be held legally responsible for the actions of  
>that user, if that user is engaging in legal activities (like software  
>piracy, cracking, etc.)?


Do you have a policy against software piracy (or some other APU)?  If so, what 
are its procedures for action?  Are they constitutional in the U.S.?  Why was 
the policy instituted?  Was it to truly protect the organization from 
liability, or just to look like a good law-and-order institution?  Have the 
provisions of the policy *ever* been used?  If so, what precedents were 
established in those previous applications of the policy?



>2)  Is there a precedence for Search and Seizure on the basis of "Probable  
>Cause"?  Under what basis does something justify "Probable Cause"?


I don't think there would be many precedents that would allow the *university* 
to violate an individual's privacy.  If, on the other hand, the university 
suspected illegal activity (such as drug dealing in a dorm room) and 
university officials wanted to do something formal (in a legal sense) they 
would turn the matter over to the proper legal authorities.  Those authorities 
do have legal means (search warrants issued by a judge) to investigate illegal 
actions in an individual's private space (as long as they have enough evidence 
to convince the judge).  The doctrine of "Probable Cause" governs searches by 
law-enforcement officers acting without a search warrant, and is designed to 
protect the officer from privacy-violation lawsuits (I think).


>3)  How would you handle the situation?  Do you have any current policies  
>that provide for search on the basis of "Probable Cause"?  Do you get a  
>legal search warrant?  Do you involve outside legal entities (FBI, NSA,  
>Secret Service, District Attorney, etc..)?  Have you actually experienced  
>this or similar situation (just general details please)?


This is probably a FAQ-type item, but it seems (IMHO) that *any* actions by 
the university that could be construed as violating an individuals *private* 
space should only be undertaken by legally authorized agents (officers with a 
warrant).  In the AUP, the nature and locations of *private* and *non-private* 
data spaces should be clearly delineated.  If a user has not been informed 
about the private/nonprivate status of their disk space, they should be 
informed in writing.  This will give them the opportunity to take appropriate 
action regarding protecting their own privacy.  Then, from that time on, 
anything that the user stores in *nonprivate* spaces might be considered as 
subject to review without notice.  Data/programs/etc in their *private* spaces 
could only be reviewed by the proper legal authorities when properly 
authorized (either consent by the user or warrant from a judge).

Informally, you might consider communicating some kind of private/nonprivate 
data space delineation to all users (especially the user in question).  
Maybe have it acknowledged in writing.  Allow some time to pass so that 
users can take appropriate actions to separate their private data from 
institutional data.  Then you would have a future basis for action.  
Personally, I would feel very uncomfortable about violating a user's private 
data space.

Just trying to help  :-)
Brian


