Our library in Boston declines some reference services in a way that is unfair because
local government public records of our library and information about how things work
behind the scenes at our library is perceived as an intrusion.

The irony is that the new president of the American Library Association is going
to be the fellow who is already our library's director, and he has participated
in the Intellectual Freedom Committee.

Representatives of our library administration and those behind the desks and counters
should be more aware of principles of intellectual freedom, how censorship can happen
in their library, Freedom of Information, public records and open meetings regulations.

Ask your library for their annual reports, city library department board minutes,
manuals, budgets, publicly funded studies about your library, and any other public
document to learn more about how things work, if you are interested.

Does anybody know if there are any books, journals or articles about
that kind of censorship in particular?

