It's incredibly boring to see the same tired old arguments being put up
by the same people against doing something serious about the bot. Now
someone is actually trying to put the work in to get something serious 
done about Argic, the old bull is crawling out of the woodwork once again.
I wouldn't bother to contradict it but for the fact that every month there
are new readers here who will not be familiar with all this total bollocks
and may be misled by it. Here, for them, is a bried FAQ on why Argic must
be got under control:


Q: Surely trying to stop Serdar Argic is against free speech, isn't it?

   A: No. 

      1. `Serdar Argic' is not a person. It is a piece of robot-posting
         software under the control of one or more people, one of whom
         is know to be Ahmet Cosar, a Turkish Grad student at cs.umn.edu.
         The robot cannot be communicated with; mail is dumped and never
         replied to directly; it is impervious to discussion or argument.

      2. Free speech implies limits. For example, at a public meeting 
	 people are not normally allowed to scream at the top of their
         voices continually so noone else gets a hearing. Argic performs
         the Usenet equivalant of this. He broadcasts continually to a 
         long list of groups; he floods those groups daily with his articles;
         he puts off new potential posters and thereby gradually kills off
         said group until `he' is virtually alone on it. The group is then
         no longer a free-speech platform but a propaganda channel for
         Mr Cosar. If you don't believe me, go take a look at soc.history.

      3. Cosar and his entity, the `Argic' robot, constantly ignore the
         free speech rights of others, trashing their postings, issuing
         false cancels, threatening them and generally attempting to
         use illegal or underhand methods to harass them off the nets.
         Does one respond to Nazism by saying `that's OK - carry on- you
	 deserve the right to harass our rights'? No. One goes to war.

      4. If we allow Argic to continue in current mode, all bots will
         be fine by precedent. The Net will gradually collapse as more
         and more bots become attached. Think Christian Fundamentalists
         are going to be happy just staying in one cosy little group?

      5. Nobody is trying to _TOTALLY REMOVE_ Cosar from the nets. We just
 	 want him to follow the well established norms of Usenet. Those
         norms are not legally defined, but they exist by consensus and
         are real.


Q: But I can just use kill files - what's the problem?


      1. A large % of sites and users have problems with this. Their sites
         may not support kill files, through no fault of their own. My
         belief is that Usenet should serve as many people as possible, 
         not just those with the best software. A very large number of
         users have to pay-by-volume for Usenet, eithor through time,
         modem-telecom charges or through time away from other activities.
         Usenet-reading is no longer just a hobbyist activity - it forms
         an important part of many people's working day. Why should people
         pay for Argic's activities? In effect, Argic (and other spammers
         like the Green Card Lawyers) are gaining massive free ad space
  	 at the expense of tens of thousands of other people.

      2. The first contact with a group is critical. An Argic-dominated
         group looks boring and incomprehensible and probably is. New 
         people just arriving at the group will be put off right away
         never to return. I have a large file full of messages from people
         who wondered where the _real_ history group was on the nets
         or statements that they tried the group and left in dismay. We
         then get the sad situation that the no 1 worldwide-intercomputer
         and interpersonal news and discussion system does not have a 
         viable group to discuss a range of significant topics. Not due
         to defects in the system or due to crassness on the part of the
         great majority of it's users. Due to the deranged activities of
         just a few people. This must change.

      3. Even on sites with good up-to-date software and no charges, killing
         all postings emanating from Argic is time-consuming and annoying,
         especially when compounded by the slowness most multi-user systems
         suffer from time-to-time. What use is Usenet if it takes minutes
         before you can first start using it every time?
	

Q: But what good will trying to stop him do? It's an anarchy isn't it?


      1. Usenet is not a total anarchy, otherwise it wouldn't function at
         all. There are well established guidelines, norms and codes
         which the great majority adhere to. (With intelligent flexibility.)

      2. The Argic postings emerge from UUNET, which is a fee-paying
         service with an administration and rules. They are in effect making
	 money from the discomfort Argic causes the rest of us. They can
	 and should be held accountable for this. 

      3. Usenet will almost certainly collapse if it's norms are totally
         abused. It will become unusable or over-regulated. It is up to
         us as users to do something about this. The best way is to 
         fiercely oppose abuses in all possible and legal ways.


Q: But lots of people on the nets behave wierdly. What's special about Argic?


      1. Argic is distinctive by the sheer volue of material it posts. There
         is no kook, bot, flamer, wierdo or hostile around who generates
         so much anger on so many groups.

      2. Almost all of the other Usenet regulars are eithor witty, amusing,
         amusingly deranged, hostile but restricted to a small range of
         groups or hostile but under control. Almost all have been amenable
         to pressure or obey netiquette to some extent. The Argic entity
         is unusual in that it never obeys netiquette, it never replies
         or heeds email or pressures, it never stops and it is wholly
         hostile and unpleasant in tone.

      
Q: So what should I do about it?



      1. If Argic infects groups you like, don't reply or post followups
         to articles posted by `him' - these only trigger more bot articles
         and so forth, adinfinitum. Don't mention keywords in your article
         like Argic, Turkey and so on as these trigger the bot.

      2. But don't give up on your fave groups. Consider moderation as one
         way to get Argic out - this was acheieved with soc.history.moderation.

      3. Sign the forthcoming Usenet petition on Argic. Details on how to
         do this will be posted to your group.

      4. Don't email Argic or Cosar directly, as they/he normally return
         the favour by mailbombing, threats or lies to sysadmins and so on,
         unless you know your sysadmin knows about Argic and you are well
         prepared.

      5. Be vigilant: bots will destrot this wonderful and unique global
         means of communication unless we all take steps to guard it.
          







