In article <2pu6ln$bfn@eff.org> kadie@eff.org (Carl M. Kadie) writes:

> I understand that there has been discussion in the Norwegian
> newsgroups about whether it is OK to created have a World-Wide Web
> page that references offensive material in another country. Is this
> correct?

Yes, more or less. There was a discussion in a national group on this
topic. It was not the University of Oslo, but I can say a few words
on the policy we have inforced here - it is similar, but not quite as
restrictive. See below.

> ...
> Has any university actually banned hypertext references to offensive
> material?

It would indeed be very interesting to know this.

> 
>  ...
> If there are any Norwegian bans, what exactly is banned?  Included
> graphics? pointers directly to an offensive picture?  pointers to
> sites that have offensive pictures? pointers to sites that have
> pointers to offensive pictures? etc?

I am now speaking on behalf of The Department of Informatics where I
am one of the sysadmins, not the whole university. 

We were probably the first major site in Norway starting to use WWW
agressively, that is - in such a way that both students and all staff
really *had* to learn how to use Mosaic and lynx, beacuse we didn't
put the information anywhere else.

Lately, we made it possible for any student at the department (~2000)
to become a, literally speaking - World Wide - Web publisher. This
was made possible by using a relatively controlled mechanism in the
NCSA httpd where http://../~user will get you to  special subdirectory
relative to that user's home directory.

Moreover, since it was interesting to see how this was used by the
students and since the students wanted to see the other students
Web pages, we automatically collected every personal home page
and made an index of them. The index is updated regularly, and linked 
into the official departmental WWW server.

     (see http://www.ifi.uio.no/ifistud.html)

(The first link on that page gets you to the page where the publishing
 rules are stated - sorry, only in Norwegian)

Now, many started to play with this and put up all kind of
interesting and not so interesting stuff on their Web pages. We 
don't *allow* people to distribute licensed software, and we
*urge* (not 'ban' ) people not to distribute or set up external 
pointers to offensive material. They seem to respect this without
any complaints.

The reason is that they are now true net-publishers, and we try 
to make them understand both which privileges and responsibilities
are involved in this. We found it natural here to distinguish
between what they publish to a potential world wide reader group,
and what kind of stuff they keep in local files which have to
be accessed by local file system mechanisms. 

More could be said about our reasons for this policy (some of it
has to do with the nature of the WWW media), but I leave it here.
I would be glad, however, to see a discussion on this.

> Also, if there is a ban, who imposed it? The computer staff?
> The Academic Senate?

The "ban", as you call it, were imposed by the system staff. Nobody
has so far complained about the decision.

	------

We truly believe in WWW around here, and are in the progress of
making it easy and safe for students to write and use HTTP
server scripts. 

