From caf-talk Caf May  1 22:06:02 1994
From: sethf@athena.mit.edu (Seth Finkelstein)
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,comp.admin.policy
Subject: Re: [comp.admin.policy]  Re: Legal Responsibility vs Privacy
Date: 2 May 1994 02:03:18 GMT
Message-ID: <2q1n16$lme@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>

In article <BETSYS.94Apr28183301@terminus.cs.umb.edu> the topic came up:
[The attribution is unclear, it is not by Elizabeth Schwartz]
>   It will be interesting to know what MIT did in regards to the current
>   case (US vs. LaMacchia) where someone had to search LaMacchia's
>   'private' directories to find the pirated software.  But we will need
>   to wait a bit as this case is in progress and no one is talking.

	Indeed, details are have not been released. But it is known that
the server for the software itself was alleged to be on free local
disk space of a public machine. Thus, the first searches were done of
a public area, not a private one. Other material might (note this is
conjecture) have established "reasonable cause" within the meaning of
the law for a search of private areas, if such a search was even done.
	It's entirely possible, even likely, that any searches were
carried out in accordance with the exact letter of the law. But that's
the not the tough part. The extremely troubling part, and which bears
very close watching, is the implication that MIT took initiative of
*soliciting prosecution* from law enforcement. As the MIT News Office
put it:

	"MIT computer specialists discovered in December that an MIT
	computer in a computer center used by students was being used to
	distribute software.  That information was turned over to the MIT
	Campus Police and the Federal Bureau of Investigation. MIT
	personnel cooperated in the subsequent FBI investigation."

	So this would seem to imply that the "recommended procedure"
is to call the local police and FBI with any evidence of wrongdoing.
No matter what the formal correctness of this, it seems extremely
unwise in practice.
	This case was flamed over in comp.org.eff.talk for a while
last month, so you might want to check the archives. I maintain that
there is a missing piece, some vital information that is not yet
public, since MIT's behavior here is atypical in the extreme. Thus, I
suggest it not be taken as a guideline, at least not until much more
information is available.

